WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.490
- Creation, transformation,
the quest for self-knowledge.

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:05.780
These are universal themes

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:07.980
that appear across world mythology.

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:10.750
But there's another common
thread within these tales:

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:12.250
gender fluidity.

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:14.740
- It's not only commonplace
in many mythologies,

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:18.470
but an essential aspect of some
important mythical figures:

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:21.110
from androgynous creators
and fertility deities

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:24.687
to warriors and prophets
that defy the gender binary.

00:00:24.687 --> 00:00:27.604
(mysterious music)

00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:31.210
- These figures challenge the belief

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:34.710
that sex and gender are
split into male and female.

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:37.300
Since ancient times,
societies have recognized

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.810
the existence of more than two genders.

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:40.910
While the language used

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:42.570
to refer to these different identities

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:45.080
and experiences varies hugely,

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:47.820
transgender, intersex,
and nonbinary people

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:49.290
have always existed

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:52.220
and played a crucial
role in world mythology.

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:55.660
So why was the ability to bend
and transcend gender sacred,

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:59.470
and what do these figures tell
us about gender in society?

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:00.880
- When it comes to creation myths,

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:03.450
many primordial deities
cannot be understood

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:05.730
as solely male or female.

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:08.800
For instance, a Babylonian
creation myth sees Tiamat,

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:11.820
or salt water, mingle
with Abzu, or fresh water,

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:13.740
to create the rest of the gods.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:16.090
The Babylonian source,
called the Enuma Elish,

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:19.930
refers to the primordial
Tiamat as both he and she.

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:22.170
However, 19th century European scholars

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:23.610
revised the original myths

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:25.640
to fit their own standards of gender,

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:27.400
referring to Tiamat as female

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:30.800
and often characterizing them
as a monstrous sea goddess.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:34.060
Similarly, the Aztec
creator deity Tlaltecuhtli

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:37.600
was described as an earth
lord and a reptilian monster

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:40.370
with feminine and masculine attributes.

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:43.160
When they waded into the sea
looking for flesh to eat,

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:45.980
the other gods ripped Tlaltecuhtli in half

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:48.260
to form the earth and the sky.

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:51.810
Much later, European
colonizers recast Tlaltecuhtli

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:53.490
as a motherly earth goddess,

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:57.150
simplifying their complex
role as a formidable protector

00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:00.140
who is also a flesh-eating destroyer.

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:02.470
Another Aztec god, Ometeotl,

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:05.310
was also a genderless agent of creation.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:09.360
Ometeotl created themself, the
universe, and the other gods,

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:12.960
and resided in the 13th
and highest heaven alone.

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.350
While some sources have framed Ometeotl

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:18.470
as a husband and wife
pairing within one god,

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:19.440
others have argued

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:21.880
that this is an overly
simplistic understanding

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:23.240
of their gender.

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:26.800
Colonial retellings often
simplified these creator deities,

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:28.080
using gendered tropes

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.870
like the fearsome lord or gentle mother.

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:33.840
But the original myths
don't conform to cliches.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:36.670
Instead, nonbinary primordial deities

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:38.960
embody the duality of creation,

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:42.390
distilling the twinned energies
of birth and destruction,

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:44.610
wonder and dread.

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:47.540
- In the Tagalog mythology of
the Philippines for instance,

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:48.730
Lakapati was worshiped

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:50.830
as a goddess of the fields and fertility

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:53.130
who was represented
with reproductive organs

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:55.420
traditionally labeled male and female.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:57.360
This representation of Lakapati's gender

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:00.520
may have associated her
with balance and abundance.

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:02.620
Similarly, certain androgynous deities

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:05.410
were associated with fertile
land in Ancient Egypt.

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:07.580
As the annual flooding
of the Nile approached,

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:10.480
the Egyptians appealed
to Hapi for rich soil.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:12.180
Hapi was seen as a fatherly figure

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:14.520
who sported the false
beard of the Pharaoh,

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:16.490
full breasts, and a pregnant belly.

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:19.070
Intersex children, who
were believed to be sacred,

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:21.500
sometimes served at Hapi's temple.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.330
Another Egyptian icon, Wadj-Wer,

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:25.850
was depicted as a
blue-green pregnant deity

00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:27.810
who protected lakes and lagoons.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:30.490
In both these figures,
fertility is not understood

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:33.400
as a purely masculine
or feminine attribute.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.140
Because the Ancient Egyptians believed

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:36.410
that men carried the fetus

00:03:36.410 --> 00:03:38.440
before transferring them to women,

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:41.090
the idea of the pregnant
man was not uncommon.

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:42.710
The concept is making a comeback today

00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:44.920
as advocates push for
more inclusive language

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:47.140
in traditionally feminine spaces.

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:48.820
When an Ancient Egyptian woman died,

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:52.080
priests temporarily transformed
her corpse into a man,

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.290
just long enough for
her to create a fetus,

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:56.760
thus ensuring rebirth in the afterlife.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:58.250
They did this by reciting spells

00:03:58.250 --> 00:04:00.310
and changing the corpse's
pronouns in prayer,

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:02.340
as well as painting its skin red.

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:04.280
The corpse then returned to female form,

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:06.090
ready to rebirth herself.

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:08.710
This ritual is one way in
which gender transformation

00:04:08.710 --> 00:04:10.820
was commonplace, and desirable,

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:13.240
in Ancient Egyptian life and afterlife.

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:15.930
- While gender transformation
was an accepted part of life

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:18.030
in some mythologies and cultures,

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:21.250
other traditions reflect a
more ambivalent approach.

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:24.020
Classical myth is full of gods and humans

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:25.790
turning into plants and animals,

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:29.230
but there are lesser-known
figures who change their genders.

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:32.350
In Ancient Greek mythology,
at the hands of various gods,

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:35.760
Tiresias' gender was
changed up to six times,

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:37.395
depending on the source.

00:04:37.395 --> 00:04:40.260
Tiresias served as a
male prophet of Apollo,

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:43.670
but also married and had
children in female form.

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:46.450
They were known for settling
disputes between the gods,

00:04:46.450 --> 00:04:48.540
like the time Zeus and Hera asked them

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:52.070
to compare the sexual
experiences of men and women,

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:55.350
and to advise famous
warriors like Odysseus.

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:56.990
Tiresias' transformations

00:04:56.990 --> 00:05:00.400
have traditionally been
depicted as godly punishments,

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:03.160
but there's another way to
read their gender fluidity.

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:06.140
Tiresias was adept at
absorbing new experiences

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:07.970
and inhabiting different roles,

00:05:07.970 --> 00:05:10.530
skills that also make them a wise prophet

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:12.560
and skilled mediator.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:13.490
Classical myths

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:16.260
offer a range of gender
identities, with figures

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:18.840
like the gender-bending
god of pleasure Dionysus

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:21.680
and the androgynous love
deity Hermaphroditus

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:24.080
celebrated in art and literature.

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:26.160
One moonlit tribute to Hermaphroditus

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:29.160
involved men and women
swapping their clothes.

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:31.620
Other sacred rites
involving dance, drinking,

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:35.150
and mind-bending substances
invited marginalized figures

00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:39.000
like women and non-Greek
citizens to express themselves.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:41.740
Scholars have noted the
existence of the galli,

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:45.210
priests who were assigned male
at birth but lived as women,

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:46.940
as a recognized group in society

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:49.560
who determined their
own gender presentation.

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:51.970
But they were a small
and privileged group,

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:55.260
and it is likely that many more
gender non-conforming people

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:58.500
have slipped through the
cracks of classical history.

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:00.310
- Still, across world mythology,

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:03.450
myths of gender fluidity testify
to the material existence

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:06.120
of many gender identities
throughout history.

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:08.700
Gender fluidity often
acts as a powerful symbol

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:11.230
for heroism and self-determination.

00:06:11.230 --> 00:06:13.810
In Philippine Suludnon lore for instance,

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:15.370
maidens known as binukots

00:06:15.370 --> 00:06:18.470
could transform into male
warriors in an emergency,

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:20.740
rescuing their friends,
searching for their husbands,

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:22.950
and engaging in righteous combat.

00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:25.920
In Hindu mythology, Shikhandi
is assigned female at birth

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:27.610
but later transforms into a man

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:30.310
with the help of a Yaksha
or benevolent spirit.

00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:32.640
Known as Shikhandi, he
becomes a great warrior

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:34.490
and works to overthrow Bhishma,

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:36.970
a king who had abducted
and humiliated him.

00:06:36.970 --> 00:06:38.900
These myths depict gender transformation

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:42.390
as a powerful model for
adaptation and spiritual growth.

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:43.580
Beyond the story of Shikhandi,

00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:46.350
Hindu cosmology offers some
of the richest understandings

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:47.870
of the gender spectrum.

00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:50.710
In the Ramayana, the god
Rama retreated into exile,

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:53.630
only for his devotees to
follow him out of love.

00:06:53.630 --> 00:06:56.040
He ordered all men and women
to return to their homes

00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:57.330
so he could be alone,

00:06:57.330 --> 00:06:59.250
but when he finally emerged from exile,

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:02.620
he found that those who did
not identify as male or female

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:04.220
had waited for him.

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:06.050
Rama blessed these apparent outcasts

00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:08.830
and gave them permission to
bless newborns in ritual,

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:11.750
thus testifying to the
importance of inclusion.

00:07:11.750 --> 00:07:12.930
Commonly called Hijras,

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:15.330
these blessed individuals
have historically been aligned

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:17.760
with fertility, music, and liminality,

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:19.650
testifying to their spiritual significance

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:20.890
in Hindu scripture.

00:07:20.890 --> 00:07:24.170
But even the divinely blessed
aren't guaranteed happy lives.

00:07:24.170 --> 00:07:26.150
While Hijra are an integral
part of the culture,

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:28.670
they often face discrimination and abuse.

00:07:28.670 --> 00:07:30.860
This shift from acceptance
to discrimination

00:07:30.860 --> 00:07:34.390
was heavily influenced by
British colonialism in India.

00:07:34.390 --> 00:07:36.480
In the 1860s and 70's for instance,

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:39.130
British settlers compiled
registers of Hijras

00:07:39.130 --> 00:07:40.870
that used derogatory language

00:07:40.870 --> 00:07:42.540
and stripped them of their rights.

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:45.610
This is just one instance of
how colonial policies and texts

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:48.580
have attacked gender
diversity across the world.

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:51.760
From revising ancient myths
to conform to binary gender,

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:53.340
to dismantling human rights,

00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:56.660
Western colonialism has sadly
imposed the gender binary

00:07:56.660 --> 00:07:59.550
on mythologies and
material realities alike.

00:07:59.550 --> 00:08:03.080
- Today, efforts to recognize
and celebrate gender fluidity,

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:06.310
in ancient myths and
modern times, continue.

00:08:06.310 --> 00:08:07.950
Ancient gender fluid deities

00:08:07.950 --> 00:08:11.340
show us that trans and nonbinary
identities are nothing new,

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:13.520
and queer and gender nonconforming people

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:16.890
continue to find community
through these mythical figures.

00:08:16.890 --> 00:08:20.190
For instance, thousands of
trans and nonbinary people

00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:24.000
partake in the Chamayavilakku
festival in Kollam each year,

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:27.480
celebrating their protector
goddess Bhagavati-devi.

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:29.300
Contemporary artists and writers

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:33.200
have also used ancient deities
to explore gender expression.

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:35.130
- Whether reclaiming the gender queerness

00:08:35.130 --> 00:08:37.370
of mythical creatures and deities.

00:08:37.370 --> 00:08:39.940
- Or subverting gender
roles in ancient myth.

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:42.260
- Or decolonizing world mythology.

00:08:42.260 --> 00:08:44.450
- These efforts emphasize the importance

00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:47.784
of representation and
expression for all genders.

00:08:47.784 --> 00:08:50.367
(gentle music)

